            <fr:title text="Greedy Kleene star via par">Greedy Kleene star via par</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Idea</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>New connective, plus a small experiment using it. Lives in
<html:code>Grammar/Par/Base.agda</html:code> and <html:code>Grammar/Par/Greedy.agda</html:code> on the
<html:code>cyk</html:code> branch of Lambek<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[^{D}]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>4</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>28</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/sss-00B8.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:title text="The connective">The connective</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>The candidate par is a per-split disjunction:
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[(A \mathbin {\mathsf {par}} B)\, w \;:=\; \forall \, s : \mathrm {Splitting}\, w.\;
  A\, s_1 \uplus  B\, s_2.]]></fr:tex>
  This is the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\forall , \uplus )]]></fr:tex> cell of a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2 \times  2]]></fr:tex> grid of
  split-quantified connectives. The other three cells are tensor
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\exists , \times ) = \otimes ]]></fr:tex>, the unnamed all-splits product
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\forall , \times )]]></fr:tex> (already in
  <html:code>Grammar/LinearProduct/AllSplits.agda</html:code> as <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\forall \otimes ]]></fr:tex>), and a
  missing <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\exists , \uplus )]]></fr:tex> cell.</html:p>
                <html:p>In a single-conclusion intuitionistic setting <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbin {\mathsf {par}}]]></fr:tex> has weak intro
  and elim — you cannot in general project a side without LEM. It
  suffices for <html:em>negation-flavored composition</html:em>: combinators like
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {refute}_\otimes  : (\neg  A) \mathbin {\mathsf {par}} (\neg  B) \vdash  \neg  (A \otimes  B)]]></fr:tex>
  go through without LEM. The full converse needs LEM.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>4</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>28</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/sss-00B8.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:title text="Greedy star">Greedy star</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Define the greedy/PEG-style Kleene star point-free as
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {Greedy}^*\, A \;:=\; {A}^{*} \otimes  \neg  (A \otimes  \top ).]]></fr:tex>
  The right factor says <html:em>no further <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> starts here</html:em>, which together
  with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[{A}^{*}]]></fr:tex> pins the prefix down to the longest <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>-run.</html:p>
                <html:p>The de Morgan dual is bidirectional in the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\otimes  \top ]]></fr:tex>
  specialization, without LEM:
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\neg  (A \otimes  \top ) \;\cong \; (\neg  A) \mathbin {\mathsf {par}} \bot .]]></fr:tex>
  The reverse direction
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\neg  A) \mathbin {\mathsf {par}} \bot  \vdash  \neg  (A \otimes  \top )]]></fr:tex> works because every
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \otimes  \top ]]></fr:tex> match supplies a split, which the par consumes; the
  forward direction works because <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\top ]]></fr:tex> is inhabited at every split, so
  per-split refutation reassembles into a global refutation.</html:p>
                <html:p>From this iso we get a stop/step pair:
  <html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {stop} : (\neg  A) \mathbin {\mathsf {par}} \bot  \vdash  \mathsf {Greedy}^* A]]></fr:tex>, packaging
    a per-split refutation into a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {Greedy}^*]]></fr:tex> with empty prefix.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {step} : A \otimes  \mathsf {Greedy}^* A \vdash  \mathsf {Greedy}^* A]]></fr:tex>,
    prepending one <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> — the tail-refutation persists since the suffix
    only shrinks.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>4</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>28</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/sss-00B8.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:title text="Concrete parser">Concrete parser</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p><html:code>Examples/GreedyA.agda</html:code> packages a greedy parser for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[{a}^{*}]]></fr:tex>
  over the alphabet <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\{a, b\}]]></fr:tex> as a <html:code>Parser (Greedy* (literal a)) ⊥</html:code>.
  Everything is point-free: the per-letter cases use
  <html:code>disjoint-ε-char+</html:code> and <html:code>same-first</html:code> to manufacture the
  per-split refutation, and the recursion is
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {fold}^*_r\, \textrm {char}\, \mathsf {nil} \, \mathsf {cons}]]></fr:tex>
  over the structure of the input string. Each <html:code>cons</html:code> step inspects
  the leading character: an <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a]]></fr:tex> extends via <html:code>step</html:code>, a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b]]></fr:tex> discards
  the recursive match and emits a fresh <html:code>stop</html:code>. The rejection grammar
  is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\bot ]]></fr:tex> — <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {Greedy}^*]]></fr:tex> is total because every word has a longest
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a]]></fr:tex>-prefix.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>4</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>28</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/sss-00B8.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:title text="Lessons">Lessons</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Three things this told us. <html:em>First,</html:em> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbin {\mathsf {par}}]]></fr:tex> is the natural shape
  for the precondition of a per-split decision procedure — exactly what a
  greedy combinator needs. <html:em>Second,</html:em> weak intuitionistic intro/elim is
  not a dealbreaker; the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\otimes  \top ]]></fr:tex> specialization is enough to
  recover the full iso, and that's what most use sites need. <html:em>Third,</html:em>
  the relationship to <fr:link href="/sss-009E/" title="Explore: Grammars and Semantic Actions Agda Code (Alexandru)" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-009E/" display-uri="sss-009E" type="local">AllSplits</fr:link>-style <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\forall \otimes ]]></fr:tex> is
  simpler than expected: the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2 \times  2]]></fr:tex> grid suggests the missing
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\exists , \uplus )]]></fr:tex> cell is also worth naming, even if its use cases
  are less obvious.</html:p>
                <html:p>Connection to the parser combinators thread on
  <fr:link href="/sss-00AN/" title="Monadic parser combinators as chains of isos, and the &quot;Dijkstra monad&quot;" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00AN/" display-uri="sss-00AN" type="local">chains of isos</fr:link>: the per-split <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\uplus ]]></fr:tex> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbin {\mathsf {par}}]]></fr:tex> is
  exactly the kind of decided-at-every-split structure that those
  combinators want internally — but here as a connective rather than as a
  termination invariant.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
